---
title: First Day Using NAHPU
sidebar:
    label: NAHPU Day 1
    order: 2
---

This guide introduces NAHPU's data model and gives you a safe place to practice
before real fieldwork or lab work. NAHPU runs on any screen, though a laptop or
tablet gives you more room while you learn the layout.

## Choose how to start

You do not have to invent practice data if you prefer working with real field
records from the start:

| Path | Time | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Practice on this page** | ~30–45 min | Learning the core concepts fast and testing your own taxon group in a temporary `NAHPU Practice` project |
| **[Basic tutorials](https://nahpu.app/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/)** | ~2 hours | Following a real 2014 Mt. Tujuh small-mammal paper catalog and field journal from project creation to a Darwin Core Archive |

{% aside type="tip" title="Prefer real field data?" %}

Go straight to the [Basic tutorials](https://nahpu.app/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/) if you
want realistic specimens, sites, events, and journal entries already prepared
for you. Come back here anytime for a quick concept overview.

{% /aside %}

## Key concepts

{% recordmap label="How NAHPU organizes field data: a project holds narratives, sites, events, and specimens, while personnel and taxa are shared across projects." hint="Select any record to see what it stores." %}
{% recordnode id="project" title="Project" summary="One body of work" detail="Groups an expedition, a monitoring program, or laboratory preparation work. Most day-to-day screens and exports operate on the active project." /%}
{% recordnode id="narrative" title="Narrative" summary="Daily field journal" detail="A day-by-day journal of the expedition. Each entry has its own date, site, and writer and records what happened that day, such as weather, logistics, permit meetings, observations that did not become specimens, or a summary of trapping results." /%}
{% recordnode id="site" title="Site" summary="A place to revisit" detail="A place you can visit again, such as a camp or trap line. Enter its geography, habitat, and one or more coordinates once and reuse it across visits." /%}
{% recordnode id="event" title="Event" summary="Work at a site" detail="One sampling session at a site during a defined period: activity, collection methods, participants, effort, and conditions. A site can have several events." /%}
{% recordnode id="specimen" title="Specimen" summary="Linked to an event" detail="Links to one event, which supplies its site and sampling context. Carries the working Field ID from its physical label plus an internal UUID. Several specimens can share one event." /%}
{% recordnode id="personnel" title="Personnel" summary="Shared" detail="People are stored in the installation database and shared across projects. Editing a person updates every project that references that person." /%}
{% recordnode id="taxa" title="Taxa" summary="Shared" detail="The taxon registry is shared across projects. To correct a single identification, update the specimen record rather than editing the shared taxon." /%}
{% /recordmap %}

### Three rules to remember

- **Enter context once (`Site → Event → Specimen`)**: reuse one site for repeat
  visits, create a new event for each sampling session, and link specimens from
  that session to the event. Keep collection dates separate from laboratory
  preparation dates.
- **Keep daily context in Narratives**: use journal entries for weather,
  logistics, permit notes, and observations that did not produce specimens.
- **Treat Personnel and Taxa as shared**: editing a person or taxon updates
  every project that uses it. To fix one misidentified specimen, change the
  specimen's identification rather than editing the shared taxon entry.

### Location, locality, and coordinates

| Term | What it records |
| --- | --- |
| **Project location** | Broad study area for the whole project |
| **Site geography** | Administrative hierarchy and specific locality description (reusable across sites) |
| **Site ID** | Short identifier for one place, such as a camp or trap line |
| **Coordinate uncertainty** | GPS accuracy for a specific point (a site can hold several points, such as one per trap or net) |
| **Specimen coordinate extent** | On the specimen form, how far the organism could have been from the selected point |

### Field IDs and UUIDs

A **Field ID** is the working identifier written on a physical specimen tag:

- **`Personnel ID`** (default): combines a Cataloger's initials and personal
  running number. Best when multiple people catalog specimens in parallel.
- **`Project ID`**: combines a project prefix, sequential catalog number, and
  suffix. Best for assembly-line preparation where one person catalogs for the
  whole project.

NAHPU follows [FAIR](https://www.go-fair.org/fair-principles/) principles and
automatically assigns internal UUIDs to specimen and symbiont records (and
optionally to specimen parts), independent of Field IDs.

### Roles and taxon identification

NAHPU separates **specimen care roles** from **event personnel roles**:

- **Specimen care roles** are set on each person and control specimen forms:
  `Cataloger` (eligible for all specimen personnel fields), `Preparator`, and
  `Determiner`.
- **Event personnel roles** describe participation in a specific collecting
  event (such as leader, student helper, local guide, or driver) and can vary
  per event.

The taxon registry defaults to species imports but accepts higher ranks such as
genus or family, so you can record an identification at the rank you can
support alongside its **identification confidence**.

### Settings, controlled vocabularies, and custom fields

- **Controlled vocabularies** populate dropdowns such as collection methods and
  preparation types; share **user configs** so every field device uses the same
  terms.
- **Catalog format** selects the active specimen form and measurement fields on
  this device. One project can still hold specimens from multiple taxon groups.
- **Custom fields** capture project-scoped or global observations that do not
  fit built-in fields.
- **Project time zone** documents the local reference time for recorded
  observations without changing the device clock or converting existing
  timestamps.

### Saving, attachments, and exports

Most form fields save automatically as you edit; dialogs, imports, and exports
ask for confirmation. Use **Narratives** for the daily field journal,
**Associated Data** for links and non-media files (links do not store offline
copies), and **Media** for images, audio, or video.

| Export | What it includes | When to use it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Project identity** | Project name, details, and UUID only | Setting up team devices so their records can merge later |
| **Project transfer** | Project records and media (`.zip` / `.tar.gz`), or records only (`Light export`, `.json.gz`) | Moving or merging one project between devices |
| **User configs** | Controlled vocabularies, field visibility, custom fields, templates, and presets | Reproducing the same setup on another device |
| **Database backup** | Full installation archive (all projects, media, and settings) | Creating a complete recovery point before imports, merges, or fieldwork checkpoints |

{% aside type="caution" title="Exports are not backups" %}

Tabular exports (CSV, TSV, Excel, JSON) and PDF documents are for review,
analysis, and printing—NAHPU cannot restore a project from them. Use a project
transfer or full database backup for recovery.

{% /aside %}

## Practice using NAHPU

Use a clearly named practice project (`NAHPU Practice`) and sample values so
exercise records stay separate from research data.

{% fieldtrail label="Practice trail with one waypoint for each practice step." hint="Select a waypoint to jump to its step. Visited steps are checked." progress="{visited} of {total} steps visited" /%}

{% steps %}

1. **Open NAHPU and explore setup**

    Open `Setup NAHPU` from the home screen menu to review catalog formats,
    Field ID modes, controlled vocabularies, and export presets—or import team
    user configs from the home screen menu, project `Dashboard` menu, or
    `Setup NAHPU`.

    **Check:** Locate the dashboard, navigation bar, and Info buttons. See
    [Settings](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/settings/).

2. **Create a practice project**

    Select `Create project` > `Create new project` and name it `NAHPU Practice`.
    Enter project details and the reference time zone, choose a catalog format
    and Field ID mode, and finish the review. (For an existing team project,
    import its project identity to share its UUID.)

    **Check:** Confirm the new project opens on the `Dashboard`. See
    [Projects](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/projects/).

3. **Add practice personnel and taxa**

    Add at least one person with the `Cataloger` role (initials and starting
    number), then register sample taxa manually by rank or import a CSV, TSV,
    or Excel file.

    **Check:** Verify proposed taxonomic ranks before confirming file or QR
    imports. See [Personnel](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/personnel/) and
    [Taxon Registry](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/taxon/).

4. **Create a practice site**

    Open `Sites` and select `+`. Enter a stable `Site ID`, administrative
    geography, and habitat description, then add one or more coordinates
    manually, from device GPS, from a file, or via QR.

    **Check:** Verify the map pin, datum, units, and coordinate uncertainty.
    Reuse this site for repeat visits. See [Sites](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/sites/).

5. **Add a practice event**

    Open `Events` and select `+`. Choose your practice site, set start and end
    dates and times (adding an Event ID suffix if multiple sessions share a
    site and date), and record activity, collection methods, event personnel,
    and weather.

    **Check:** Try duplicating the event to see that setup copies over while
    environmental measurements stay blank. See
    [Collecting Events](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/events/).

6. **Create a sample specimen record**

    Open `Specimens` and select `+` using a practice label. Verify the
    `Field ID`, assign `Cataloger`, `Preparator`, and `Determiner`, pick a
    taxon and `ID Confidence`, link your collecting event, and enter
    measurements, specimen parts, symbionts (for vertebrate catalog formats),
    and sample `Media` or `Associated Data`.

    **Check:** Reopen the specimen record to confirm every section saved. See
    [Specimen Records](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/specimens/).

7. **Review records and write a narrative**

    Cross-check Field IDs, counts, linked events, coordinates, and attachments
    against your practice labels, then open `Narratives` to write a short daily
    entry and preview the rendered Markdown.

    **Check:** Try `Export records` for a review table and `Export documents`
    for printable labels or catalogs. See [Narratives](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/narrative/)
    and [Export](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/export/).

8. **Practice project transfer and recovery**

    Export `NAHPU Practice` as a `.zip` or `.tar.gz` archive (or `Light export`
    `.json.gz` without media) and inspect any warnings. On a second device,
    test `Import project` from the home screen or `Merge project` when the
    project UUID already exists there.

    **Check:** Always create a database backup before importing or merging, and
    never overwrite a live research database for practice. See
    [Project transfer and recovery](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/export/project-transfer/).

{% /steps %}

## Check your understanding

Before starting field or lab work, make sure you can:

- explain how `Site → Event → Specimen` enters each context once;
- identify whether your project uses `Personnel ID` or `Project ID`;
- fix a single specimen's identification without editing the shared taxon;
- choose between sharing project identity, transferring a project, exporting
  user configs, and creating a full database backup.

## Next steps

- Follow the [Basic tutorials](https://nahpu.app/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/) using a real
  2014 paper field catalog from project setup to a Darwin Core Archive.
- Learn to [configure custom fields](https://nahpu.app/en/how-to-recipes/prepare/configure-custom-fields/).
- [Browse the Cookbook](https://nahpu.app/en/how-to-recipes/) for quick task recipes.
